{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1168673-how-much-research-is-enough-or-too-much","id":1168673,"title":"How much research is enough-or too much?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-12-18T16:55:59-08:00","id":1168673,"title":"How much research is enough-or too much?","content":"Hey all, I'm working on my very first Chronicles of darkness campaign-specifically changeling, but I feel that my query applies to every CofD game. I'm building my setting based on the town I'm currently living in, which is big enough to be a city but isn't for reasons. What I want to know is, how much geographical knowledge do you guys recommend when it comes to setting a chronicle in a city? How much do you feel I should know before starting out, even if i end up fleshing out more as time goes on? My only other experience with building a setting is with the Chicago citybook, which gave me a lot of info to start with. Beyond that, I feel like I might go overboard with my ideas of how much I feel I should know, especially since it ends up being the biggest obstacle when it comes to running games.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7372-lady-gray","name":"Lady Gray","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7372_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-18T17:51:49-08:00","id":1168686,"title":"","content":"Well, what are the key interesting spots around the area? Where is most of the action going to take place? Are there plausible reasons Changelings might not go to certain areas? If so, you can flesh those out later, if you need to.<br /> <br /> Since this is for a specific game (as oppossed to a setting for others) I'd focus on what is immediately relevant, then build out from there as needed.<br /> <br /> As for level of detail, use index cards (or similar small box note spaces) to list for each area: key feature(s), feel/theme/mood, and at least one &quot;face&quot; NPC. Equipment bonuses, regional Aspirations, Conditions/Tilts, and the like are nice optional additions.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":7212,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","user":"Vent0","date":". 12-18-2017, 08:56 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-18T18:49:18-08:00","id":1168698,"title":"","content":"This is pretty ambiguous, but somewhere between 'enough that you have a recognizable foundation to work off of,' but before 'no one's going to notice this but you.'<br /> <br /> Building a game-relevant setting is important but it's easy to go wild and start adding detail after detail that might not be noticed or won't have any effect on the game. That's also an easy way to get burned out, or build yourself into a corner. Sometimes you need a certain kind of building or setting or organization and it helps if you haven't already established that it doesn't exist.<br /> <br /> But the foundation would really help in regards to confidence when actual gameplay, being able to answer questions simply and smoothly, like who's the mayor, is there a gas station or lake near here, where's Elysium or the border of the Black Moon eXtreme territories. I thought the Chicago book was great, but it offers a lot of information I never used and I'm guessing others didn't, either, though there's probably some 'ones trash is anothers treasure there,' but having a history about areas and figures of import as opposed to things that won't be too relevant to the game can help forging plot details and characters. These things can be more or less important depending on what game is being played.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 12-18-2017, 10:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-18T19:42:13-08:00","id":1168703,"title":"","content":"It also depends on the style of the campaign you're planning to run. For a very open, sandbox-y game, where players might go anywhere/try anything, it'll pay off to have a bunch of stuff in your pocket just in case you <i>might </i>end up using it. For a campaign that's on a tight plot rail, you can ignore stuff that's not going to intersect with the plot, or detail it very lightly. Most campaigns fall somewhere between the two extremes, of course.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13641-mad_maudlin","name":"Mad_Maudlin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"229"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-19T10:57:47-08:00","id":1168855,"title":"","content":"Knowing basic history of area is good - if your city/region have Wikipedia page - read it. If no - use some areas tourists guide. Then read all references from Wiki page - well, up to twenty or so. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> You need to understand general history of region you are playing - but <b>do not <i>over-interest</i></b> on it. You want to have open mind sometimes too much real world lore can overburden you with.<br /> <br /> <i>Then</i> concentrate on what PCs will interact with. If they will have warehouse in the port as base - read about this port district. If they are having night club on collage town - like I have in my modern <b>Werewolf</b> game - read on universities history of region. And a like.<br /> <br /> In short - <i>Know general history of city</i>, but concentrate only on things your players will interact. Or will interest you as ST to incorporate from general history. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> More you will run the setting, more things about area you will discover later on in chronicle. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-19T13:13:17-08:00","id":1168897,"title":"","content":"And decide how how you and the players feel about deviations -- I mean, you have supernatural elements working here in the WoD, so naturally things will be different. If it serves your game to have a dojo on a particular corner instead of the coffee shop that is currently in residence, you should be able to do that. When I've played games in familiar territory, I've often had places where that cool building that burned down in 1950, didn't. Or the rich family I made up decided to build a large library where currently there are dingy apartments. If you're fine with that, and the players are too, then that gives you lots of room to maneuver. Also, it saves you from a mistake that could cramp your plot. Is it important to your plot that a drug deal goes bad in the parking deck next to the players' apartment... but a player points out the parking deck is actually two blocks away? &quot;Well, in the game, there was legal wrangling and they ended up developing across from your apartment building.&quot; You players will probably just drop it and go with the flow. If not, well, you could always hint that there's an interesting reason why this ground was torn up or that ground wasn't, and make a plot out of it (Someone was trying to destroy a locus here, or protecting the secret crypt of a vampire where the garage is in real life).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2203-corrigan","name":"Corrigan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"189"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}